# PART 12 — GOODS CUSTODY AND LIABILITY POLICY

This is a critical part of the application.

Distinguish between:

A. Physical custody of goods.

B. Financial debt.

C. Contractual liability.

These are related but are not automatically identical.

## 12.1 Agent custody policy

Provide two configurable modes.

### Mode A — Consignment / Entrusted goods with accountability

This is the default initial mode.

The agent receives goods from the warehouse and signs a receipt confirming:

- Product
- Quantity
- Unit reference value
- Total reference value
- Date
- Responsibility for safekeeping
- Responsibility for documenting retailer deliveries
- Responsibility for returning undelivered stock or resolving discrepancies according to the agreement

The goods are recorded as inventory under the agent's custody.

Their reference value is shown in the agent's custody statement.

The transfer does not automatically create an immediately payable cash debt unless the applicable agreement defines such liability.

### Mode B — Credit sale to the agent

The owner can explicitly choose a credit-sale model.

Under this model:

- Goods are sold to the agent on credit.
- The full agreed amount becomes a monetary receivable at delivery.
- An installment or settlement deadline is recorded.
- Payments reduce the agent's monetary debt.
- The agent becomes responsible for the goods as buyer under the applicable agreement.

The selected policy must appear clearly on the signed document.

Never silently switch between these modes.

## 12.2 Retailer delivery mode

Similarly, retailers must support:

- Consignment
- Credit sale

Default: Consignment.

In consignment mode, reported sales generate retailer receivables.

In credit-sale mode, the agreed delivered quantity generates receivables immediately.

The mode is stored with every delivery.

